## Codex Tool Usage

- Pass an explicit `workdir` to shell command tools.
- Use `rtk` for token-heavy shell output when it is available and supports the command: `rtk git status`, `rtk git diff`, `rtk rg`, `rtk pytest -q`, `rtk cargo test`.
- Use `rtk proxy` only when raw output is required.
- For unsupported syntax, use the native command; do not force an incompatible rewrite.
- A `PreToolUse` hook denies `cat` of files over 350 lines. Read a bounded range with `sed -n`, or delegate the reading with `bulk-read` as described in the `my-bulk-read` skill.
- Use skills for occasional multi-step workflows so their detailed instructions load only when relevant.

## Codex Subagent Routing

- Before delegating, read the `my-subagent` skill for execution conditions, role selection, dispatch, and review procedures.
- Default to `fork_turns="none"` when calling `spawn_agent` and include the required context in the task prompt. Inherit only the minimum necessary turns when the task requires conversation history.
- Every time you call `wait_agent`, explicitly set `timeout_ms` to twice the estimated remaining completion time in milliseconds, within the tool's minimum and maximum limits. If you cannot estimate it, explicitly use the configured default of `120000` ms. Completion notifications end the wait early, so do not shorten it for status polling. After a timeout, update the estimate and apply the same rule.
